Server Components can be `async`, so just await your data source directly — a database query, ORM call, or fetch. There's no useEffect, no useState, and no client-side waterfall. Because the fetch happens on the server, credentials stay secret and the response HTML already contains the data, so there is no loading spinner on first paint.
export default async function Page() {
const product = await db.product.findUnique({ where: { id: 'p1' } });
return <h1>{product.name}</h1>;
}

Interview tip: Show that you avoid useEffect for data fetching in App Router.

Treat every Server Action like a public endpoint. Validate inputs with Zod (or similar) at the top of the function and reject invalid shapes. Always re-check authorization inside the action — the session is server-side truth, and the client can be forged. Use the Origin/Host header check Next.js performs plus SameSite cookies for CSRF protection, and never trust hidden form fields for identity.
'use server';
export async function createItem(input: unknown) {
const data = Schema.parse(input);
const user = await requireUser();
await db.item.create({ data: { ...data, userId: user.id } });
}

Interview tip: "Never trust the client" is the phrase; also mention rate limiting.

Create a `route.ts` file inside a route folder and export one function per HTTP method. Each function returns a `Response` and receives a NextRequest (plus params for dynamic segments like /products/[id]). There is no catch-all handler, and methods you don't export simply return 405 Method Not Allowed. Use NextResponse.json() when you need cookies or headers set on the outgoing response.
export async function GET(req: NextRequest, { params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
const item = await db.item.findUnique({ where: { id: params.id } });
return item ? Response.json(item) : Response.json({ error: 'Not found' }, { status: 404 });
}
export async function POST(req: Request) {
const body = await req.json();
return Response.json({ id: '1', ...body }, { status: 201 });
}

Interview tip: Note that you export one function per method — no single catch-all handler.

A stale closure is when an effect's callback captures an old value from a previous render — you see the pre-update state. Fix it with the functional update form (setCount(c => c + 1)) or by listing the value in the dependency array. Race conditions occur when an earlier async response arrives after a later one and overwrites fresher data. Guard with an `ignore` flag set in the effect's cleanup, or use an AbortController to cancel the superseded request.
useEffect(() => {
let ignore = false;
fetchData().then(d => { if (!ignore) setData(d); });
return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [id]);

Interview tip: The cleanup function is the single mechanism that solves both — emphasize it.

A custom hook is a function starting with use that packages reusable stateful logic — it is just a regular function that may call other hooks. It lets you share behavior (not markup) across components while keeping each component's state independent. Example: useDebouncedValue(value, delay) returns a value that updates only after the user stops typing, so you can call an API once instead of on every keystroke.
function useDebouncedValue<T>(value: T, delay = 300) {
const [v, setV] = useState(value);
useEffect(() => { const t = setTimeout(() => setV(value), delay);
return () => clearTimeout(t); }, [value, delay]);
return v;
}

Interview tip: Note the naming rule — use prefix is required for linting and for React's rules-of-hooks checks.

any turns off type checking for a value and defeats inference — treat it as a last resort. unknown is the safe top type: you can hold anything but must narrow before using it. never is the bottom type, meaning no value is possible; it is perfect for exhaustive checks and functions that only throw. void means the function returns nothing meaningful, which is not the same as undefined.
function fail(msg: string): never { throw new Error(msg); }
function log(): void { console.log('hi'); }

Interview tip: Use unknown instead of any for untrusted input, and put never in a default branch to prove a switch is exhaustive.

A union (A | B) means the value can be either shape; the compiler only allows access to properties common to every member. An intersection (A & B) means the value satisfies both shapes at once, combining their properties. Unions model states and errors, intersections model composed entities.
type Status = 'idle' | 'loading' | 'error';
type Authed = User & { token: string };

Interview tip: Mention narrowing with in, typeof, or instanceof before touching union-specific members.

Generics are type parameters — placeholders that let one component or function work with many types while preserving the relationship between input and output. The generic argument is inferred from the call site, so callers rarely specify it explicitly.
interface ApiResponse<T> {
data: T;
message: string;
status: number;
}
type UserResponse = ApiResponse<User>;
async function get<T>(url: string): Promise<ApiResponse<T>> { /* ... */ }

Interview tip: Contrast generics with any: generics keep type safety while avoiding casts, and inference removes the boilerplate.

A discriminated union is a union of object types that share one literal field — the discriminator. Switching on it lets TypeScript narrow the union, so you can only access fields valid for that branch, with no casts. Adding a new variant later then produces a compile error everywhere it must be handled.
type Result =
| { status: 'ok'; data: User }
| { status: 'error'; error: string };
function render(r: Result) {
switch (r.status) {
case 'ok': return r.data.name;
case 'error': return r.error;
default: { const _: never = r; return _; }   // exhaustive
}
}

Interview tip: Combine the discriminator with never in the default branch so missing cases surface at compile time.

Strict mode is the strict: true compiler flag that turns on the strictest checks. It enables strictNullChecks (the biggest win — null and undefined must be handled explicitly), noImplicitAny, strictFunctionTypes, strictBindCallApply, strictPropertyInitialization, useUnknownInCatchVariables, and alwaysStrict. It catches real bugs early but demands an explicit null-handling style.
{ "compilerOptions": { "strict": true, "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true } }

Interview tip: Enable strict from day one in a new codebase; in legacy code, turn the flags on one at a time to keep the migration manageable.

Give every component an explicit Props type, annotate handlers with React's event types, and use a type parameter for generic components so callbacks receive a correctly typed row. Server Actions take a typed input and return a typed result; Route Handlers use the Request/Response web types and declare their JSON payload.
type Props = { items: Item[]; onSelect: (id: string) => void };
function List<T>({ rows, render }: { rows: T[]; render: (r: T) => React.ReactNode }) {
return <ul>{rows.map((r) => <li key={r.id}>{render(r)}</li>)}</ul>;
}
const onChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => setValue(e.target.value);

Interview tip: Show the Server Action and Route Handler signatures too, e.g. export async function GET(req: Request): Promise<Response>.

The cleanest approach is a shared contracts package in a monorepo — packages/contracts — holding API DTOs, Zod schemas, and error types, imported by both the Next.js app and the API. Alternatives are publishing to a private npm registry or generating types from an OpenAPI spec. Never import server-only modules into client code, or secrets leak into the browser bundle.
// packages/contracts/src/user.ts
export const UserSchema = z.object({ id: z.string().uuid(), name: z.string() });
export type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;

Interview tip: Derive the static type from the runtime schema with z.infer so the two can never drift apart.

Types vanish at build time, so anything from the network is unknown until parsed. Use a runtime schema library such as Zod (or Valibot, Joi) at the boundary: parse on the server, then pass an already-typed value inward.
const result = UserSchema.safeParse(await res.json());
if (!result.success) {
throw new ApiError('Invalid API payload', result.error.issues);
}
return result.data;   // typed as User

Interview tip: Use safeParse for expected failures and parse for programmer errors, and log validation issues with the request id.

Model the response as a union of two shapes sharing a boolean or literal discriminator, so callers must handle both branches. Using ok: true / ok: false gives a clean early-return style that narrows without casts.
type ApiResult<T> =
| { ok: true; status: number; data: T }
| { ok: false; status: number; error: { code: string; message: string } };

Interview tip: Return errors as data rather than exceptions when the caller can reasonably continue, and pair this with a schema for the payload.

Model absence explicitly as string | null | undefined, use optional chaining and ??, and avoid the ! non-null assertion, which only silences the compiler. Environment variables are string | undefined and arrive as text, so parse them at startup with a schema and export typed constants.
const Env = z.object({
DATABASE_URL: z.string().url(),
SENTRY_DSN: z.string().optional(),
NODE_ENV: z.enum(['development', 'test', 'production']),
});
export const env = Env.parse(process.env);   // fails fast on boot

Interview tip: Only prefix client-visible variables with NEXT_PUBLIC_, and never expose secrets to the browser.

Define roles as a literal union and permissions as another, then map roles to permissions with Record. A helper checks access, and the compiler flags typos or a missing role entry. Keeping the map exhaustive means adding a role is a compile error until you decide its permissions.
type Role = 'admin' | 'editor' | 'viewer';
type Permission = 'post:create' | 'post:delete' | 'post:publish';
const PERMISSIONS: Record<Role, Permission[]> = {

Interview tip: Client-side checks are UX only — enforce exactly the same rules on the server.

