Interview tip: Avoid destructuring the whole store — const { items } = useCart() re-renders on any change.

Update the cache immediately in onMutate, snapshot the previous data, and restore it in onError. Call cancelQueries first so in-flight reads cannot overwrite your optimistic value. Always invalidate in onSettled to resync with the server afterwards.
onMutate: async (vars) => {
await qc.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['items'] });
const prev = qc.getQueryData<Item[]>(['items']);
qc.setQueryData<Item[]>(['items'], (old = []) => [...old, vars]);
return { prev };
},
onError: (_e, _v, ctx) => qc.setQueryData(['items'], ctx?.prev),
onSettled: () => qc.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['items'] }),

Interview tip: Reserve optimistic UI for fast, low-risk mutations — never for payments or anything requiring confirmation.

The event loop takes one task from the macrotask queue (timers, I/O, events), runs it to completion, then drains the entire microtask queue (promise callbacks, queueMicrotask) before the next macrotask. Microtasks always beat macrotasks, and an await continuation is also a microtask.
console.log('A');
setTimeout(() => console.log('B'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('C'));
// A -> C -> B

Interview tip: Ask them to predict the order of then vs await vs setTimeout — it exposes whether they truly understand the two queues.

A Promise is an object representing a future settled value, with .then, .catch, and .finally. async marks a function that always returns a Promise. await unwraps a Promise and pauses that function only — the thread stays free — and also works on plain values. try/catch catches rejections when awaiting.
async function load() {
try {
const res = await fetch(url);
return await res.json();
} catch (e) {
throw new Error('load failed', { cause: e });
}
}

Interview tip: Sequential awaits in a loop create a waterfall — use Promise.all for independent requests.

Promise.all waits for all and rejects fast on the first error. Promise.allSettled never rejects and returns each result's status. Promise.race settles with the first result, fulfilled or rejected. Promise.any resolves with the first fulfillment and rejects with AggregateError only if every promise rejects.
const results = await Promise.allSettled(urls.map((u) => fetch(u)));
const ok = results.filter((r) => r.status === 'fulfilled');

Interview tip: Use allSettled for partial-failure UI, and any for redundant sources where the first success wins.

Use an AbortController and pass its signal to fetch, then call abort() from a cleanup function or when newer input arrives. Axios accepts signal as well, and TanStack Query hands the signal to your queryFn. Cancelling frees the browser connection but may not stop server-side work, so APIs should remain idempotent.
useEffect(() => {
const ac = new AbortController();
fetch(url, { signal: ac.signal }).then(setData);
return () => ac.abort();
}, [url]);

Interview tip: Mention AbortController.timeout(ms) for a request deadline.

A closure is a function bundled with a reference to its lexical scope. The inner function keeps the outer variables alive, which is exactly why callbacks and React state updaters still observe old values.
function counter() {
let n = 0;
return () => ++n;   // closure over n
}

Interview tip: This is the root of the stale-closure problem in useEffect — fix it with the dependency array or a ref.

Hoisting moves declarations to the top of their scope: var becomes undefined, function declarations are fully available, and let/const exist but sit in the temporal dead zone, throwing a ReferenceError until initialized. var is function-scoped; let/const are block-scoped; functions create their own scope.
console.log(a); // undefined
var a = 1;
console.log(b); // ReferenceError
let b = 2;

Interview tip: The TDZ explains the confusing "Cannot access 'b' before initialization" error.

Every object has a hidden __proto__ link to another object; property lookup walks that prototype chain until it finds the key. JavaScript's class syntax is layered over this mechanism, with extends and super wiring the chain. Object.create builds an object with a chosen prototype, and instanceof checks the chain.
class Animal { speak() { return 'noise'; } }
class Dog extends Animal { speak() { return 'woof'; } }
new Dog().speak();  // 'woof'

Interview tip: Contrast with class-based languages: JavaScript delegates along the chain rather than copying members.

Debouncing waits for the input to stop for a fixed delay before running the function — ideal for search boxes and validation. Throttling runs at most once per interval, executing immediately then ignoring further calls — ideal for scroll and resize handlers.
const onScroll = throttle(() => updateLayout(), 100);
const onSearch = debounce((q) => fetchResults(q), 300);

Interview tip: Debounce delays work until the burst ends; throttle drops work during the burst. Ask which one the scenario needs.

Retry transient failures (network errors, 5xx, 429) with a delay that doubles each attempt, capped and jittered so clients do not resynchronise. Only retry idempotent operations, and always cap the number of attempts.
async function withRetry<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, retries = 3): Promise<T> {
for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
try { return await fn(); }
catch (err) {
if (attempt >= retries) throw err;
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 2 ** attempt * 100 + Math.random() * 50));
}
}
}

Interview tip: Also mention honouring the Retry-After header on 429 responses.

Clean up everything an effect creates: clearInterval, clearTimeout, removeEventListener, unsubscribe, abort() a fetch, observer.disconnect(), and clearing stores. Return a cleanup function from useEffect. Because React 18 Strict Mode mounts effects twice in development, cleanup must be idempotent and safe to run twice.
useEffect(() => {
const id = setInterval(poll, 5000);
return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);

Interview tip: Leaks typically surface as growing listener counts, a steadily increasing timer count, or a "cleanup function changed" warning.

Run a bounded pool: keep at most N requests in flight and start the next one as soon as one settles. This protects rate limits and the browser's per-host connection cap while still parallelising the work.
async function pool<T, R>(items: T[], limit: number, fn: (i: T) => Promise<R>): Promise<R[]> {
const out: R[] = [];
let idx = 0;
const worker = async () => {
while (idx < items.length) {
const i = idx++;
out[i] = await fn(items[i]);
}
};
await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker));
return out;
}

Interview tip: Mention the p-limit package and that firing 1000 Promise.all requests at once exhausts connections and triggers throttling.

A decorator is a function that wraps another function to add behaviour without editing its body. It takes a function and returns a new function. Common uses: timing, logging, retry, caching, auth checks. In FastAPI, @app.get("/chat") registers a route; in LangChain, @tool turns a function into an agent tool. Example:
def logged(fn):
def wrapper(*a, **kw):
print("call:", fn.__name__)
return fn(*a, **kw)
return wrapper

A generator is a function that yields values lazily instead of building a whole list in memory. Calling it gives a generator object; values are produced only as you iterate. That keeps memory flat for large data — ideal for streaming LLM tokens or reading huge files line by line. Example:
def chunks(text, size=1000):
for i in range(0, len(text), size):

Use try / except / else / finally. except catches named exceptions, else runs when nothing failed, finally always runs for cleanup. Catch specific exceptions rather than a bare except: so real bugs are not hidden. GenAI code must handle provider errors such as RateLimitError or APIConnectionError and retry them. Example:
try:
answer = llm.invoke(prompt)

Exponential backoff doubles the wait after each failure — 1s, 2s, 4s — with random jitter so many clients do not retry at the same instant. Retry only transient errors: 429, 500, 502, 503 and network timeouts. Never retry 400 or 401. Cap the attempts (usually 3) and the maximum delay. Example:
for attempt in range(3):
try:
return await call()

Dependency injection (DI) means a function receives its collaborators as arguments instead of constructing them internally. That keeps code testable — inject a fake LLM client in tests — and swappable. FastAPI supports DI natively with Depends:
def get_client() -> OpenAI:
return OpenAI(api_key=settings.api_key)
@app.post("/chat")
def chat(req: ChatRequest, client: OpenAI = Depends(get_client)):

